  "source_document": "A bounded queue stores pending work up to a fixed capacity. When the queue is full, backpressure makes producers wait instead of creating more queued work. Consumers remove queued work when they have capacity. Backpressure limits pending work; it does not make consumers process faster.",
  "document_sha256": "b51aed7a273db2331dac207cbbc94da89389183e4405b3b6a94d75a7e7b59a7e",
  "prior_answer": "1. A bounded queue holds pending work up to a fixed capacity. [SOURCE_4c9dff036cbd1022258cb37d]\n2. Backpressure makes producers wait when the queue is full; it does not make consumers faster. [SOURCE_4c9dff036cbd1022258cb37d]",
  "question": "Explain the second point again with a simple everyday analogy.",
  "answer": "The second point is: \"Backpressure makes producers wait when the queue is full; it does not make consumers faster.\"\n\nThink of it like a restaurant with a limited number of tables. When the restaurant is full, the waiters (producers) can't take in any more customers (queued work) because the tables are already full. They can't just magically create more tables to accommodate the waiting customers. Instead, they have to wait until a table becomes available for a new customer.\n\nSimilarly, in a queue, when it's full, the producers (tasks) have to wait until space becomes available before they can continue processing. It doesn't make the consumers (tasks that are already processing) work faster; it just means the producers have to wait their turn.",
